(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-03"] = {"chem-11-03-m01":{"idea":"Atoms bond to become stable. Noble gases are stable because their outer shell is full, and most atoms copy this.","steps":["Noble gases (except helium) have 8 electrons in the outer shell.","Other atoms gain, lose or share electrons to reach the same 8.","This is the octet rule."],"others":"Two electrons is the duplet of helium, true only for very small atoms like H. Ten electrons is not a noble gas outer shell for any period. One electron is the state of an alkali metal, which tends to lose it.","trap":"Octet = 8 (oct- means eight).","answer":"eight electrons in its outer shell, like a noble gas"},"chem-11-03-m02":{"idea":"An ionic bond needs one atom that loses electrons easily and another that gains them easily.","steps":["The first atom must have low ionisation enthalpy, so it gives up an electron cheaply.","The second atom must have a high negative electron gain enthalpy, so it takes the electron and releases energy.","The ions then attract each other strongly."],"others":"Equal electronegativity gives a covalent bond, as no atom pulls harder. Two atoms with high ionisation enthalpy both hold electrons tightly. Two atoms that both resist gaining electrons will not transfer them either.","trap":"Metal gives (low IE), non-metal takes (high negative EGE).","answer":"an element with a low ionisation enthalpy and one with a high negative electron gain enthalpy"},"chem-11-03-m03":{"idea":"Lattice enthalpy measures how strongly the ions are held in a crystal.","steps":["Ions in a gas come together and form a solid.","Energy is released when 1 mole of the solid forms from its gaseous ions.","A larger value means a more stable crystal."],"others":"Adding an electron to a gaseous atom is electron gain enthalpy. Breaking covalent bonds in a gas is bond dissociation enthalpy. Removing one electron from a gaseous atom is ionisation enthalpy.","trap":"Lattice = gaseous ions to solid.","answer":"released when one mole of the solid is formed from its gaseous ions"},"chem-11-03-m04":{"idea":"Formal charge shows how many electrons an atom owns in the structure compared with its free atom. Bonding electrons are shared equally.","steps":["Formal charge = (valence electrons) − (non-bonding electrons) − (bonding electrons)/2.","Non-bonding electrons belong wholly to the atom.","Of the bonding electrons, the atom owns half."],"others":"Non-bonding minus bonding has no valence term. Valence − bonding − (non-bonding)/2 swaps the two halves, which is wrong. Adding all three terms would give a large positive number that has no meaning.","trap":"Half of the bonding electrons, all of the lone electrons.","answer":"valence electrons − non-bonding electrons − (bonding electrons)/2"},"chem-11-03-m05":{"idea":"Formal charge tells whether an atom has more or fewer electrons than its free atom.","steps":["Nitrogen has 5 valence electrons.","It has no lone pair. It forms one double bond and two single bonds, so 8 bonding electrons.","FC = 5 − 0 − 8/2 = 5 − 4.","FC = +1."],"others":"0 would need 5 bonds, which nitrogen cannot make with 8 electrons. −1 would mean it holds a lone pair. +2 would need six or fewer electrons, which does not match the structure.","trap":"Check: the total formal charge in NO₃⁻ is −1, since +1 on N and −1 on two O.","answer":"+1"},"chem-11-03-m06":{"idea":"VSEPR stands for valence shell electron pair repulsion. Electron pairs keep as far apart as possible.","steps":["Electron pairs around the central atom repel each other.","They arrange themselves to be far apart.","This arrangement gives the shape of the molecule."],"others":"Atom size alone does not decide the shape. Nuclei attraction is not the main factor. Neutrons do not affect shape, because they are in the nucleus.","trap":"Pairs repel, so shape follows.","answer":"the repulsion between the electron pairs in the valence shell of the central atom"},"chem-11-03-m07":{"idea":"Count the electron pairs on the central atom, then place them as far apart as possible.","steps":["Be has 2 valence electrons, which make 2 bonding pairs with Cl.","Be has no lone pairs.","Two pairs get farthest apart on opposite sides, giving a straight line.","So the shape is linear and the angle is 180°."],"others":"Bent at 104.5° needs two lone pairs, as in water. Trigonal planar at 120° needs three pairs. Tetrahedral at 109.5° needs four pairs.","trap":"Two pairs and no lone pair: always linear.","answer":"linear, with the angle Cl–Be–Cl of 180°"},"chem-11-03-m08":{"idea":"The shape depends on both bonding pairs and lone pairs. Lone pairs take space but are not seen in the shape name.","steps":["N has 5 valence electrons. Three make bonds with H.","The other two form one lone pair.","Four pairs give a tetrahedral arrangement, but one corner is a lone pair.","The atoms form a trigonal pyramid."],"others":"Tetrahedral with no lone pair describes CH₄. Bent with two lone pairs describes H₂O. Trigonal planar with no lone pair describes BF₃. Nitrogen in NH₃ has one lone pair.","trap":"Four pairs with one lone pair: pyramidal.","answer":"trigonal pyramidal, because of one lone pair on nitrogen"},"chem-11-03-m09":{"idea":"Lone pairs repel more than bonding pairs. They push the bonds closer together.","steps":["Oxygen in water has 4 pairs: 2 bonding and 2 lone.","The ideal tetrahedral angle is 109.5°.","Lone pair repulsion is stronger than bond pair repulsion.","The lone pairs squeeze the H–O–H angle down to 104.5°."],"others":"Atom size alone does not explain the squeeze. The H atoms do not repel more strongly than lone pairs. Water bonds are polar covalent, not ionic.","trap":"Order of repulsion: lp–lp > lp–bp > bp–bp.","answer":"the two lone pairs on the oxygen repel the bonding pairs more strongly"},"chem-11-03-m10":{"idea":"Six bonding pairs with no lone pairs spread out evenly in three-dimensional space.","steps":["SF₆ has 6 bonding pairs and no lone pair.","The pairs point to the corners of an octahedron.","All S–F bonds are alike and every neighbouring angle is 90°."],"others":"Trigonal bipyramidal is for five pairs, with angles of 90° and 120°. Tetrahedral is for four pairs. Square planar needs six pairs with two lone pairs, as in XeF₄.","trap":"6 pairs, 0 lone: octahedral.","answer":"octahedral, with all the bond angles of 90°"},"chem-11-03-m11":{"idea":"A sigma bond is the strongest type of covalent bond. Orbitals overlap head on.","steps":["In a σ bond, the orbitals overlap along the line joining the two nuclei.","This end-to-end overlap is large, so the bond is strong.","The electron density sits between the nuclei."],"others":"Sideways overlap of p orbitals above and below the axis is a pi bond. Electron transfer makes an ionic bond. Overlap with an empty orbital describes a coordinate bond, not the definition of sigma.","trap":"σ = head-on. π = side-on.","answer":"the overlap of the orbitals along the line joining the nuclei"},"chem-11-03-m12":{"idea":"A single bond is one σ. A double bond is one σ plus one π.","steps":["Ethene is H₂C=CH₂.","It has 4 C–H bonds, all σ.","The C=C double bond has one σ and one π.","Total σ = 4 + 1 = 5. Total π = 1."],"others":"4 σ and 2 π miscounts the C=C bond. 6 σ and 0 π treats the double bond as having two σ bonds. 3 σ and 3 π has far too many π bonds for a molecule with one double bond.","trap":"Only the first bond between two atoms is σ.","answer":"5 sigma bonds and 1 pi bond"},"chem-11-03-m13":{"idea":"Hybridisation mixes atomic orbitals to give equal new orbitals. The shape depends on how many orbitals mix.","steps":["Carbon in CH₄ has 4 σ bonds and no lone pair.","Four orbitals are needed: one s and three p give four sp³.","They point to the corners of a tetrahedron.","The angle is 109.5°."],"others":"sp with 180° is for two electron groups. sp² with 120° is for three groups. sp³d with 90° is for five groups.","trap":"Count the groups: 4 means sp³.","answer":"sp³ hybridised, with the bond angle of 109.5°"},"chem-11-03-m14":{"idea":"The percentage s character is the fraction of s orbital in the hybrid.","steps":["sp² mixes one s with two p orbitals.","Total orbitals = 3.","s character = 1/3 × 100 = 33.3%."],"others":"50% is for sp. 25% is for sp³. 66.7% is the p character of sp², not the s character.","trap":"sp = 1/2, sp² = 1/3, sp³ = 1/4.","answer":"33.3 percent"},"chem-11-03-m15":{"idea":"Bond order tells how many bonds join two atoms. In molecular orbital theory it comes from counting electrons.","steps":["Bond order = (Nb − Na) / 2.","Nb is the number of electrons in bonding orbitals.","Na is the number of electrons in antibonding orbitals.","Antibonding electrons cancel bonding electrons."],"others":"Counting only bonding electrons ignores the cancelling effect. Twice the antibonding electrons has no meaning here. Adding bonding and antibonding electrons gives the total electrons, not bond order.","trap":"Half of the difference.","answer":"half of the difference between the number of electrons in the bonding and the antibonding orbitals"},"chem-11-03-m16":{"idea":"Use the electron count and the molecular orbital order to find the bond order and whether electrons are paired.","steps":["N₂ has 14 electrons.","Bonding electrons Nb = 10 and antibonding electrons Na = 4.","Bond order = (10 − 4) / 2 = 3.","All electrons are paired, so N₂ is diamagnetic."],"others":"Bond order 2 is for O₂, not N₂. Paramagnetic means unpaired electrons, but N₂ has none.","trap":"N₂ has a triple bond and is diamagnetic.","answer":"3, and the molecule is diamagnetic"},"chem-11-03-m17":{"idea":"A molecule is paramagnetic when it has unpaired electrons.","steps":["O₂ has 16 electrons.","The last two electrons go into the two π* orbitals, which have equal energy.","By Hund's rule, they stay separate with parallel spins.","So there are two unpaired electrons in antibonding π* orbitals."],"others":"Bonding π orbitals are full in O₂, so they have no unpaired electrons. The σ orbital is also full. No unpaired electrons would make O₂ diamagnetic, which does not match the experiment.","trap":"O₂ is attracted by a magnet, a famous proof of MO theory.","answer":"two unpaired electrons in the antibonding π* orbitals"},"chem-11-03-m18":{"idea":"A higher bond order means a stronger and shorter bond.","steps":["More bonding electrons pull the atoms closer.","So bond length decreases.","The bond is harder to break, so bond energy increases."],"others":"Increasing length with increasing energy goes against the trend. Length that stays constant is false. Decreasing length with decreasing energy is also wrong, as shorter bonds are stronger.","trap":"Triple bond: shortest and strongest.","answer":"decreases, and the bond energy increases"},"chem-11-03-m19":{"idea":"Dipole moment measures how much a bond is polar. It depends on charge and distance.","steps":["Dipole moment μ = Q × r.","Q is the size of the charge on one end.","r is the distance between the two charges."],"others":"A sum of charge and distance mixes different units. A ratio of charge to distance is upside down. Charge times the square of distance has no basis here.","trap":"Product, not sum or ratio.","answer":"the product of the charge and the distance between the two charges, μ = Q × r"},"chem-11-03-m20":{"idea":"Dipole moment is a vector. A molecule can have polar bonds and still be non-polar overall.","steps":["Each C=O bond is polar, because O is more electronegative than C.","CO₂ is linear, so the two bond dipoles point in opposite directions.","They are equal in size, so they cancel.","Net dipole moment = 0."],"others":"Carbon is less electronegative than oxygen, not more. A bent shape would give a non-zero moment. The C=O bonds are polar, not non-polar.","trap":"Linear and symmetric means the dipoles cancel.","answer":"the molecule is linear, and the two bond dipoles cancel each other"},"chem-11-03-m21":{"idea":"A molecule has a net dipole moment only if its bond dipoles do not cancel.","steps":["NH₃ is pyramidal with a lone pair on N.","The three N–H dipoles do not cancel because the shape is not flat.","So NH₃ has a net dipole moment."],"others":"CO₂ is linear, so its dipoles cancel. CCl₄ is a symmetric tetrahedron, so its dipoles cancel. BF₃ is flat and symmetric, so its dipoles also cancel. High electronegativity alone is not enough.","trap":"Check the shape, not just the polar bonds.","answer":"NH₃, because it is pyramidal and the bond dipoles do not cancel"},"chem-11-03-m22":{"idea":"Resonance is used when one Lewis structure cannot describe the molecule correctly.","steps":["Several Lewis structures can be drawn for the same molecule.","The atoms stay in the same places.","Only the positions of electrons (double bonds, lone pairs) differ.","The real molecule is a hybrid of these structures."],"others":"Structures with different atoms are different molecules. A single structure with all electrons in lone pairs is not resonance. The structures do not change into each other in time, because the real molecule is always the hybrid.","trap":"Same atoms, different electron positions.","answer":"two or more Lewis structures that differ only in the positions of the electrons"},"chem-11-03-m23":{"idea":"In resonance, the real structure is an average. All equivalent bonds are the same.","steps":["Ozone has two resonance structures: O=O–O and O–O=O.","In the hybrid, both O–O bonds are partly double.","So both bond lengths are equal."],"others":"One single and one double would be true only for a single fixed structure. The central atom being more electronegative does not make bonds different. Ozone is bent, not linear.","trap":"Equal bond lengths are the proof of resonance.","answer":"equal, because of resonance"},"chem-11-03-n01":{"idea":"Bond order = (Nb − Na) / 2. Fill the 18 electrons into the molecular orbitals in energy order.","steps":["Order: σ1s, σ*1s, σ2s, σ*2s, σ2p, π2p (two), π*2p (two), σ*2p.","18 electrons fill up to the π* orbitals completely.","Nb = 2 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 10 (σ1s, σ2s, σ2p, π2p).","Na = 2 + 2 + 4 = 8 (σ*1s, σ*2s, π*2p).","Bond order = (10 − 8) / 2 = 1.0."],"trap":"O₂²⁻ has a single bond, like the O–O in peroxides.","answer":"1.0"},"chem-11-03-n02":{"idea":"Find Nb and Na from the filled molecular orbitals, then use bond order = (Nb − Na) / 2.","steps":["N₂⁺ has 13 electrons.","Orbitals filled: σ1s², σ*1s², σ2s², σ*2s², π2p⁴, σ2p¹.","Nb = 2 + 2 + 4 + 1 = 9. Na = 2 + 2 = 4.","Bond order = (9 − 4) / 2 = 2.5."],"trap":"N₂ has bond order 3. Removing one bonding electron gives 2.5.","answer":"2.5"},"chem-11-03-n03":{"idea":"Formal charge = valence electrons − non-bonding electrons − half the bonding electrons.","steps":["Nitrogen has 5 valence electrons and no lone pairs.","Bonding electrons: one double bond (4) and two single bonds (4) = 8.","FC = 5 − 0 − 8/2 = 5 − 4.","FC = +1."],"trap":"The −1 charges sit on the single-bonded O atoms. Their total with +1 on N gives −1 for the ion.","answer":"+1"},"chem-11-03-n04":{"idea":"Percentage ionic character = (observed dipole moment / dipole moment if fully ionic) × 100.","steps":["Observed μ = 1.03 × 3.336 × 10⁻³⁰ = 3.436 × 10⁻³⁰ C m.","For a full charge: μ = e × r = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 127 × 10⁻¹² = 2.032 × 10⁻²⁹ C m.","Ratio = 3.436 × 10⁻³⁰ / 2.032 × 10⁻²⁹ = 0.169.","Percentage ionic character = 16.9%."],"trap":"Convert pm to m (10⁻¹²) and D to C m before dividing.","answer":"16.9%"},"chem-11-03-n05":{"idea":"Dipole moments are vectors. Add the two O–H dipoles using the angle between them.","steps":["Resultant μ = 2 × μ_bond × cos(θ/2).","θ = 104.5°, so θ/2 = 52.25°. cos 52.25° = 0.612.","μ = 2 × 1.51 × 0.612.","μ = 1.85 D."],"trap":"The answer is less than 2 × 1.51 = 3.02 D, as the dipoles are not in line.","answer":"1.85 D"},"chem-11-03-n06":{"idea":"Count every single bond as one σ. In double and triple bonds, only one bond is σ.","steps":["C–H bonds: CH₃ has 3, CH= has 1, =CH has 1, ≡CH has 1. Total 6.","C–C σ bonds: C₁–C₂, C₂=C₃ (one σ), C₃–C₄, C₄≡C₅ (one σ). Total 4.","Total σ = 6 + 4 = 10."],"trap":"The three π bonds (one from the double bond, two from the triple bond) are not counted.","answer":"10"},"chem-11-03-n07":{"idea":"Steric number = number of bonded atoms + number of lone pairs on the central atom.","steps":["Xe has 8 valence electrons. Four are used to bond with four F atoms.","The remaining 4 electrons form 2 lone pairs.","Steric number = 4 + 2 = 6."],"trap":"Six pairs give an octahedral arrangement, but the shape is square planar.","answer":"6"},"chem-11-03-n08":{"idea":"Enthalpy change = energy to break bonds − energy released when bonds form.","steps":["Bonds broken: 1 H–H (436) + 1 Cl–Cl (243) = 679 kJ.","Bonds formed: 2 H–Cl = 2 × 431 = 862 kJ.","ΔH = 679 − 862 = −183 kJ."],"trap":"A negative sign shows heat is released. More energy comes out than goes in.","answer":"-183 kJ"}};
